### Are streams faster than for-loops?

No, streams are not inherently faster. The JIT compiler optimises both aggressively. The choice should be based on readability and correctness first, performance second.

### When does each win?

| Scenario | Winner | Reason |
|----------|--------|--------|
| Sum 10 integers | for-loop | Zero overhead vs pipeline setup |
| Sum 1M integers | Comparable | JIT optimises both |
| filter+map+reduce 1M | Comparable | Fusion compensates overhead |
| Parallel filter+reduce 1M | parallelStream | Multi-core distribution |
| LinkedList parallel | for-loop | LinkedList splits in O(n) |
| Primitive array sum | for-loop | No autoboxing |

### How do you avoid autoboxing?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class PrimitiveStreamDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] amounts = {1000, 2000, 3000, 4000, 5000};

        // WRONG: autoboxing with Stream<Integer>
        // int sum = Arrays.stream(amounts).boxed().reduce(0, Integer::sum); // boxing!

        // CORRECT: IntStream avoids boxing
        int sum = IntStream.of(amounts).sum(); // pure primitives
        System.out.println("Sum: " + sum);
    }
}
```

```text
Sum: 15000
```

### When does parallelStream help?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class ParallelBenchmark {
    // Simulate CPU-bound work
    static BigDecimal monteCarlo(int seed) {
        BigDecimal result = BigDecimal.ONE;
        for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
            result = result.add(BigDecimal.valueOf(Math.sin(seed + i)));
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Sequential: processes one at a time
        long seqStart = System.nanoTime();
        IntStream.range(0, 10_000).mapToObj(ParallelBenchmark::monteCarlo).toList();
        long seqTime = (System.nanoTime() - seqStart) / 1_000_000;

        // Parallel: distributes across cores
        long parStart = System.nanoTime();
        IntStream.range(0, 10_000).parallel().mapToObj(ParallelBenchmark::monteCarlo).toList();
        long parTime = (System.nanoTime() - parStart) / 1_000_000;

        System.out.println("Sequential: " + seqTime + "ms");
        System.out.println("Parallel: " + parTime + "ms");
    }
}
```

### When does parallelStream hurt?

parallelStream hurts for small datasets, I/O-bound work, and poorly splittable sources.

| Source | Splittability | Parallel benefit |
|--------|--------------|------------------|
| ArrayList, array | Excellent (O(1) split) | High |
| HashSet | Good | Medium |
| TreeSet | Moderate | Medium |
| LinkedList | Poor (O(n) split) | Low or negative |
| Stream.iterate | Poor (sequential by nature) | Negative |

```java
// WRONG: parallelStream on a small dataset
List.of(1, 2, 3).parallelStream().sum(); // overhead > benefit

// WRONG: parallelStream on I/O-bound work
accounts.parallelStream()
    .forEach(a -> httpClient.post(a)); // contention on connection pool

// WRONG: parallelStream on LinkedList
LinkedList<Integer> ll = new LinkedList<>();
ll.parallelStream().filter(n -> n > 0).toList(); // O(n) per split
```

### How do you benchmark correctly with JMH?

```java
import org.openjdk.jmh.annotations.*;

@BenchmarkMode(Mode.AverageTime)
@State(Scope.Thread)
public class StreamBenchmark {
    private List<Integer> data;

    @Setup
    public void setup() { data = IntStream.range(0, 100_000).boxed().toList(); }

    @Benchmark
    public int forLoop() {
        int sum = 0;
        for (int n : data) sum += n;
        return sum;
    }

    @Benchmark
    public int streamSum() { return data.stream().mapToInt(i -> i).sum(); }

    @Benchmark
    public int parallelSum() { return data.parallelStream().mapToInt(i -> i).sum(); }
}
```

System.nanoTime is unreliable due to JIT warmup, GC, and dead-code elimination. JMH handles all of these.

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: using parallelStream by default "because it's faster"
accounts.parallelStream().filter(Account::active).toList(); // unnecessary overhead for small data

// CORRECT: default to sequential, parallel only after profiling
accounts.stream().filter(Account::active).toList();

// WRONG: benchmarking with System.nanoTime without JIT warmup
long start = System.nanoTime();
result = accounts.stream().filter(f).toList();
System.out.println(System.nanoTime() - start); // unreliable!
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Default to streams for multi-step pipelines because readability is more valuable than nanoseconds in 99% of code.
- Use for-loops when you need primitive performance, index access, or mutation.
- Use parallelStream only after profiling confirms it helps.
- The real optimisation tool is JMH, not System.nanoTime.
- The common ForkJoinPool is shared across the JVM; a slow parallel stream can starve other tasks.
